    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§2807-j & 2807-t, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5553\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the public health law, in relation to authorizing the\ncommissioner of health to extend the use of audit findings to subsequent\nun-audit periods with regard to payments to patient service providers\nand assessments on covered lives\n\nPURPOSE:\nTo allow the Commissioner of Health the authority to enter into\nmulti-year HCRA settlement agreements.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 of the bill would amend section 2807-j of the Public Health\nLaw to authorize the Commissioner of Health to enter into agreements\nwith designated providers of services and third-party payors, using\nprevious audit findings and applying such audit findings for\nsubsequent un-audited periods of time.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend section 2807-t of the Public Health\nLaw to authorize the Commissioner of Health to enter into agreements\nwith designated providers of services and third-party payors, using\nprevious audit findings and applying such audit findings for\nsubsequent un-audited periods of time.\n\nSection 3 - Provides this act would take effect immediately.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe bill would authorize the Department of Health to enter into\nmulti-year settlement agreements with third-patty payors and Article\n28-certified entities (hospitals and clinics) related to HCRA\nsurcharge assessments. Currently, the Department of Health is\nauthorized to enter into multi-year settlements for years through\n2009. The multi-year settlement authority allows the Department of\nHealth and third-party payers to take the audit findings from a\ndefined audit\nperiod and apply such findings to unaudited future periods. These\nsettlements have proven beneficial to the State, third-party payors\nand Article 28 entities by allowing payors or entities to pay HCRA\nassessments without having to expend the time and resources necessary\nto conduct full audits.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nThis is a new bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nThis bill may result in additional HCRA revenue in 2012.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nShall take effect immediately, with provisions.\n\n",
